The file below was changed with the intent described. Which other file(s) in this repository must change alongside it?

Changed file: internal/contextindex/parse.go

Intent:
contextindex: extract Go symbols from the AST, and record what a grammar refuses

goSymbols was a line-regex scanner with no lexical state. Measured over the
3,477 first-party Go files of the Beamfall workspace against a go/parser truth
set it emitted 53,859 symbols against 48,012 real ones: 10,180 false positives
(18.9% of its own output) and 4,333 false negatives (9.0% of the real set), and
no counter anywhere could observe either number. Three defect classes account
for nearly all of it -- a function-local `var` promoted to a top-level symbol,
every member of a parenthesized `const (...)` / `type (...)` group missed, and a
`func` written inside a backtick raw string emitted as a real declaration. All
three are consequences of reading Go a line at a time; none is fixable by a
better regular expression.

goSymbols now walks the go/parser AST. Over the same corpus it is exact: 48,012
emitted, 48,012 real, 0 false positives, 0 false negatives, and 0 sources the
parser refused. Over Corvint's own 737 Go files -- the corpus Corvint indexes when
it dogfoods itself -- the scanner was 1,292 false positives and 1,803 false
negatives; that is now 0 and 0.

The parse-failure path is the point of the change as much as the accuracy is.
A refused source used to be indistinguishable from a source with no symbols,
which is the defect class this index is most exposed to: extraction that stops,
emits nothing, and leaves the file counted as indexed. goSymbols now returns the
refusal reason, goImports returns the one it already had and was discarding, and
Index.Unparsed records both. A receipt surfaces them when the list is non-empty
-- and only then, so a receipt over a repository whose sources all parse stays
byte-identical and the field never becomes a structurally-zero key nobody reads.

The non-Go import path keeps its regular expression, but stops presenting its
output as equivalent: sourceImports returns an importExtraction that marks
scanner-derived edges approximate, and Index.ApproximateImports counts the
sources they came from. Over the workspace's 979 tracked JS/TS files, 896 carry
5,343 such edges.

`.claude` joins forbiddenParts. No repository in the workspace tracks one today,
so nothing currently indexed changes; the screen sees only tracked paths. It
holds for a repository that commits an agent worktree, whose files are copies of
its own source rather than first-party code.

TestBuildEvalAllocationRatchet is re-baselined: the AST costs 106.3 MB/op
against the scanner's 33.4 MB/op over its 14.2 MB corpus. The bound it used to
carry was tight enough to also catch a restored eager blob read; that invariant
is pinned structurally, and more directly, by
TestBuildEvalFetchesNoBlobForACleanRepository.

Verified: go build, go vet, gofmt clean; ./internal/contextindex/... and
./cmd/corvint/... green; conformance/cli-parity-v0 green in 315.6s --
SUMMARY parity=100 accepted-divergences=3 known-divergences=3
location-normalizations=13 structural-fields=1 unsupported-refusals=5
full-gpk-v0-005=PARTIAL detached-descendants=NOT_RUN. No divergence to
adjudicate.

