The file below was changed with the intent described. Which other file(s) in this repository must change alongside it?

Changed file: internal/pythongrammar/native.go

Intent:
pythongrammar: stop rejecting `type(...)` statements and real lambda parameters

The two remaining Python false-positive classes, re-derived rather than
inherited: over 3,567 git-tracked .py files in the 77 workspace repositories the
closed grammar refused 9, and CPython 3.12.14 `ast.parse` accepts all 9. A
refused file loses EVERY symbol and import it defines, which c0e2c88 made
countable through Index.Unparsed. Both classes are now closed; the corpus
measures 0.

Class A -- the soft keyword `type`. statement() routed every statement whose
first token was `type` into the PEP 695 alias rule, which then demanded a NAME
second token. `type(self).calls += 1` is not an alias, so the whole file died on
it. The reported shape ("augmented assignment whose target opens with a call")
was a coincidence of the corpus: `type(x)`, `type(self).calls`, `type(a) ==
type(b)`, `type.mro(x)` and `type = 5` were refused too, while the structurally
identical `obj.attr().field += 1` always passed. TypeAliasStart now reproduces
CPython's own commitment rule -- `type_alias: "type" NAME [type_params] '='
expression` -- and both the grammar route and the validation route consult it, so
one predicate replaces two divergent ad-hoc guards.

Class B -- lambda parameters. lambdaParameters admitted one bare NAME per comma
group, so every default, `*args`, `**kwargs`, `/` and bare `*` was malformed;
`f(lambda *_args: invoked.touch())` is what the corpus actually carries.
A lambda's list is CPython's `lambda_params`, which differs from a def's `params`
only in taking no annotation -- and an annotation colon is unreachable here,
SpanColon having already ended the span at the first top-level colon. So the
existing parameters() rule, which already encodes the full ordering discipline,
judges it, and the partial reimplementation is deleted. One sibling defect fell
out of that: DanglingUnary read a lambda's keyword-only `*` marker as a dangling
star expression, refusing `f(lambda a, *, b: a)`. It now skips each lambda's
parameter span; `f(*)` and `f(x, *, y)` are still refused.

GPK-V0-033 adjudication for both classes: go-defect. The oracle path for these
symbols is ast.parse (src/context_corvint_index.py:1092-1095), and CPython 3.12.14
accepts every rejected form. The candidate contradicts the observable the oracle
defines, so Go is repaired and src/ is untouched. No `python-defect` reading is
available -- the oracle is not wrong about its own grammar -- and no `spec-gap`,
because DR-0010 already records this exact direction ("conservatively rejects
valid Python") as an adjudicated go-defect against the closed grammar.

Regression tests fail on c0e2c88's sources and pass here, each pinning the
reported form, the corpus form, and the siblings that already passed, so a fix
cannot over-widen. Two guard tests pin the other direction: the def parameter
orderings CPython refuses stay refused, as do dangling stars and a keyword alias
name. A before/after differential over 24 invalid forms moves nothing on the
validated route the index walks.

conformance/cli-parity-v0 passes against a binary built from this tree.

